.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV-PUT-ITEM 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv-put-item \- write an item
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekv put-item
.BR \-\-table | \-t
.I TABLE
.RB { \-\-item | \-i
.I JSON
|
.B \-\-item\-file
.IR PATH }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Writes an item, replacing whatever was stored under its key.
.PP
.B It replaces rather than merges.
Writing
.I {"supplierId":"4711","name":"x"}
over a record of twenty attributes leaves a record of two. There is no
partial update yet, so changing one field means reading the item,
editing it and writing it back \- and two of those racing will lose one
of the edits.
.PP
The item is an ordinary JSON object and may nest as deeply as you like.
It has to carry the table's key attributes with the types the table
declared; anything else is yours. Types survive the round trip: a
number comes back a number,
.I 3
does not become
.IR 3.0 ,
and an empty object stays an empty object.
.PP
Attribute names may not be empty, start with
.I $
or contain
.IR . " \-"
refused here rather than escaped, so that what you read back is exactly
what you wrote. Values may not nest more than 32 deep.
.PP
An item's
.I _created
is set the first time it is written and left alone afterwards;
.I _modified
moves on every write. Both are the server's, taken from its clock.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table " " \fITABLE\fR
Table to write to. Required.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-item " " \fIJSON\fR
The item, as a JSON object.
.TP
.BR \-\-item\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
A file holding the item as JSON. Use this for anything you would not
want to quote into a shell twice. Give one of this and
.BR \-\-item ,
not both.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The item was written; it is printed back as stored.
.TP
.B 1
No such table, the item was not an object, it was missing a key
attribute or carried one of the wrong type, an attribute name could not
be stored, or the JSON did not parse.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv put-item --table suppliers --item-file supplier.json

euclid-cli ekv put-item --table deliveries --item \e
    '{"supplierId":"4711","deliveredAt":"2026-09-10T22:00:00Z","files":7}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Changing one field of an existing record, whole:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv get-item --table suppliers --key '{"supplierId":"4711"}' \e
    | jq '.active = false | del(._created, ._modified)' > supplier.json
euclid-cli ekv put-item --table suppliers --item-file supplier.json
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekv (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-get-item (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-delete-item (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-create-table (1)
